# REAL lines from the USO MikroTik lab (dropnook/UnraidSecretaryOffice#2), 2026-10-09 (CEST), as Unraid 7.3.3's own
# syslog server (rsyslog, UDP 514, server_filename syslog-%FROMHOST-IP%.log) wrote them on the test server Tower.
# Router: MikroTik RouterOS CHR 7.24.5 (stable), the raw image from mikrotik.com (SHA-256 checked against the download
# page), licence level «free», a KVM VM on isolated libvirt networks only (ether1 «wan» 10.77.1.0/24 with libvirt's DHCP
# as the ISP, ether2 «lan» 10.9.9.0/24 with a second CHR «lab-client» as the fake client, ether3 «mgmt» 10.77.3.0/24 where
# Tower is 10.77.3.1 — Tower's SSH sessions and the syslog path run there). Identity lab-chr, time zone Europe/Zurich.
# Not scrubbed: every address and MAC here is the lab's own (10.77.x, 10.9.9.x, 10.66.0.x, 10.0.0.x, 10.88.0.x,
# 52:54:00:4D:54:xx); users admin, lab (a second full user), alice (L2TP), wan-user (PPPoE); the SSH key is the lab's.
# Lines «uso-step …» are the lab's own markers (/log info), what follows each marker is what that step provoked.
# Lines starting with # are this header (the tests leave them out).
# Action (System → Logging → Actions): remote=10.77.3.1 remote-port=514 remote-protocol=udp remote-log-format=syslog
#   syslog-time-format=bsd-syslog syslog-facility=local0 add-topics-string=yes (7.24.5's default is add-topics-string=no:
#   then the topics are NOT in the line — see mikrotik-7.24.5-chr-notopics.log); rules topics=info/warning/error/critical.
# Steps: 1 «hello unraid» · 2 logins via console (local), ssh password, ssh key, api, rest-api, ftp, telnet · 3 failures
# (6× ssh, an unknown user, rest-api, telnet — whose second attempt typed a command as the user name —, console; 3b api,
# rest-api, ftp after a pause: RouterOS itself throttles a burst — «too many concurrent logins» — and logs nothing then)
# · 4 a second user «lab»: filter/nat/mangle/raw rules added, changed, moved, removed, an address list entry, an address,
# the identity changed and back (the header shows the new identity at once), ether2 disabled and enabled («device
# changed», NO link line), REST and API changes («by api:lab@::» / «by api:lab@<addr>») · 5 ether2 link down/up from
# the hypervisor, three quick flaps · 6 DHCP server lab-dhcp, the client's lease, release (deassigned/assigned), a static
# lease · 7a WireGuard handshake + ping: nothing logged but the config lines · 7b L2TP (alice in/out), L2TP/IPsec, a
# wrong password · 8 firewall: the guide's log rules (limit=10,20:packet works on 7.24.5) — input TCP from Tower, a
# log=yes drop without prefix (UDP), forward ICMP from the client; 8b a 30 s scan: 333 drop-wan-in lines for 606
# probes (≈ 10/s, the limit), cut to the first 30 here; 8f a forward UDP line (connection-state:invalid) · 9 WAN loss:
# ether1 link down 130 s (dhcp-client «client1» lost/got) · 10 clean reboot, unclean (virsh destroy), «cannot set time
# before package build time» (2020 refused), the clock set to Sep 30 and Mar 1 2027 (the header follows the router's
# clock: skew), a hypervisor clock 20 days behind (the CHR kept the right time — not reproducible on a CHR) · 9c–9f
# PPPoE against the second CHR as server: connect, link loss, «failed to authenticate ourselves to peer», disable.
# Skew: the router's clock ran ≈ 1–3 s behind Tower's (NTP pointed at Tower, which doesn't serve the lab networks).
